Chapter 10, Section 29: "The Gray Star Mission"



――Everything had been proceeding without a hitch.


Beneath a grand chandelier suspended from the ceiling, its light blazing brilliantly, the graceful tones of string instruments filled the great hall. Guests clad in richly colored formal attire chatted and laughed, cups in hand.

The evening banquet, held in the great hall of the manor of Viscount Disken Morrow—the lord who governed the surrounding region—was overflowing with the voices of numerous invited guests, presenting the very picture of prosperity.


The guests were all of impeccable standing: nobles with close ties to Viscount Morrow, merchants who held major trade routes through the territory, and notable figures whose influence spanned multiple villages.

The viscount himself, serving as host, raised his cup and offered prayers for the peace and prosperity of his domain. Voices of assent rose in response, and the orchestra encouraged a sense of unity with a resplendent tune.


Everyone cast their approval upon Disken Morrow's words, never doubting that he was on their side.

That was only natural. –Including the very fact of their invitation here, the guests had been provided with ample recompense, and had been made to thoroughly savor the benefits of contributing to Viscount Morrow's domain.

When there is so much to gain, human beings can naturally overlook a certain degree of incongruity. –They bore no malice, no consciousness of wrongdoing. Unwitting collaborators—that was the ideal.


Viscount Disken Morrow had, of late, greatly altered his manner of governance.


Until recently, Viscount Morrow had been conservative—or, to put it unkindly, possessed of a cowardly nature that feared change. However, over the past several months, Viscount Morrow's attitude had deviated from his previous stance of maintaining the status quo, and he had begun to launch several major initiatives that drew in the influential figures of the surrounding area.

Past fifty, enjoying wine and fine food, protecting his secure position—this had once been derided as Viscount Morrow's way of life. The change, which made such mockery seem like a lie, was greatly welcomed by those who had profited from it.


Tonight's celebration had been held precisely under the pretext of inviting those who had gained from the most recent major transaction, and showing them gratitude for their cooperation without reserve.


Having passed beyond middle age and approaching his elder years, Disken Morrow's brilliance had truly blossomed. Such was the assessment of Viscount Morrow in this gathering.


――For none could possibly know that Disken Morrow had been replaced by another person entirely, his position stolen root and branch.


"――――"


Raising a glass of wine to his lips, the person calling himself "Viscount Morrow" quietly surveyed the banquet.

Responding to the words cast his way, answering consultations with a smile, and praising those who praised him even more in return.

In doing so, he thoroughly confirmed the spread of his "poison."


"Poison"――it did not mean that he had literally administered a lethal substance.

To put it another way, the "poison" was the very self that called itself Disken Morrow and deceived others about its position. It slowly ate away at its victims, and before they knew it, even laid hands upon their lives. The "poison" administered for that purpose was, he had heard, now spreading throughout every corner of the kingdom.

The reason it was hearsay was that he himself did not know the details of the plan to spread the "poison." But it could not possibly be only him—the effects could be quietly sensed when observing the nation's condition.


Most likely, the time when the "poison" would truly take effect was near.

For that impending moment, there was a duty to remove every obstacle. –That was precisely why, despite their appearances being absolutely identical, the longtime retainers and servants who doubted the truth of what was inside had to be made to disappear, all together.


Invitations had been sent to them as well.

However, these were not invitations to the glittering banquet unfolding before his eyes, but rather specified entry to a separate building through the back servants' entrance, under the pretext of a discussion for explanation and reconciliation.

Everyone had already arrived and been guided to the manor's basement. And by the time the orchestra finished its final piece, they would quietly leave the mansion. –As corpses.


The bodies would be loaded onto a dragon carriage, arranged to appear as though they had been attacked by bandits along the highway.

They had failed to repair their relationship with their lord and had left the banquet in indignation. Afterwards, the words of "Viscount Morrow"—pitifully lamenting that the discussions with his retainers had broken down—would be accepted with words of comfort by those present, who would then learn of the tragedy that followed.


Once tonight was over, all discord would vanish from this territory. That was the true purpose of the "poison."

The person calling himself "Viscount Morrow" was certain of this, and returned a smile to the flattery of a merchant who approached.


"My, truly, what a splendid banquet. By the way, my lord, have you spoken with that couple over there?"


At the end of the merchant's gaze, within the circle of guests, stood a man and a woman.

They were said to be husband and wife, but what first caught the eye was the wife.

Her abundant black hair was elegantly bound up, her white skin adorned with tasteful cosmetics, and her evening gown was refined and well-coordinated. The dark-colored dress flared out dramatically toward the hem, further accentuating her slender, tightly-cinched waist and abundant curves.

The woman held an open fan to her lips, chatting amiably with an elderly noblewoman beside her. The way she moved her gaze, her every gesture—each was flawless and complete, not merely beautiful, but revealing a deep understanding of how she appeared to others and how to conduct herself to please them.


――Natsumi Schwartz.


Indeed, a young lady who had inherited vast assets from her father's sudden death—so the guest registry stated. But she had been added hastily through letters of introduction from another domain, making her one of the few new faces at tonight's gathering that was supposed to have been composed entirely of allies.


Beside her stood her husband—a tall figure with deep green hair tied back in a single bundle.

He wore formal attire that was not excessively ornate, and at his waist hung a decorated longsword. He appeared to come from a martial family, with evidence of training visible in his posture and gait, but what particularly drew the eye was his countenance—the eyepatch over his left eye, which made his handsome features all the more striking. The absence, paradoxically, served to highlight the beauty that remained. He was a person who left such an impression, and together with his wife, they made a well-matched couple who did not lose to one another.


"Oh, I had the pleasure of speaking with them just earlier,"

said a lady carrying the scent of perfume, noticing the lord's gaze and cutting into the conversation. She was apparently fond of gossip, revealing a curious smile as she spoke.


"They said they're traveling around various regions seeking investment opportunities for the wife's fortune."


"It seems others are also taking an interest. I, of course, am one of them, but I thought it might be somewhat improper to approach them before the host, Viscount Morrow, himself."


He responded to the lady's mischievous gaze and the merchant's self-important words with a practiced smile. But even in the short time that followed, talk of the Schwartz couple reached his ears repeatedly.

It was just as the name "Schwartz" was being firmly carved into the mind of "Viscount Morrow."


"――I am called Natsumi Schwartz. Please forgive my rudeness in greeting you so late."


Thus, before Natsumi Schwartz, who offered an elegant greeting with a bow, "Viscount Morrow" nodded magnanimously, concealing behind his smile the sharp eyes of one appraising his prey.


"We are both grateful, my husband and I, to have been invited to such a splendid occasion this evening. This territory is wonderfully vibrant. It is surely proof that your lordship's governance is broadly accepted by the people."


Following his wife's greeting, the husband also offered a polite bow, then praised "Viscount Morrow's" capable governance.

Needless to say, he had no shortage of prepared responses. The couple were new faces, but they seemed quite talkative, and he had gleaned information in succession from the guests who had spoken with them.

However, as "Viscount Morrow" considered which card to play, the husband's single utterance—"But"—closed his mouth, serving as a preface to an important matter.


"We are in a position of managing assets entrusted to us from my wife's family. We cannot make rash judgments based solely on what meets the eye."


"We have heard some things in passing, but we understand that your lordship has been advancing several new enterprises within the territory. If it would be possible, might we hear more of the details?"


Matching her husband's slightly lowered tone, the wife broached the main subject.

Indeed, the approach to the matter had clearly been discussed thoroughly between the couple. In that case, even if he were to deflect the main topic with roundabout words, it would only waste unnecessary time for both parties.

They would change locations, talk, and the Schwartz couple too would come to bear a portion of the duty of spreading this "Viscount Morrow's" "poison." –Concerns would vanish, and his cards would increase. What a fine day.



"――Thank you so much for making time for us, Viscount."


He did not even allow his own guards into the parlor where he invited the couple. It was a necessary concession, given the Schwartz couple's earnest request.

At any rate, with only a single thick door separating them from the bustle of the great hall, it was just the three of them—"Viscount Morrow" and the couple.


"Over the past several months, we have heard that this territory has been undergoing very active changes, with the expansion of highways and the construction of new markets. 'Stagnation is the same as decline'—the Schwartz house has been taught by my father that change and growth are the path to prosperity."


Wearing a wry smile at the wife's words, which made no attempt to hide her ambitious, gleaming nature, "Viscount Morrow" poured wine into three glasses with his own hands and took his seat. Leaving the wine untouched, the husband continued his wife's words with, "Please do not take offense,"


"I, like my wife, sense great potential in this Morrow domain. In order to believe in that potential and take action ourselves, there are several things I would like to confirm."


The husband sought to seize the initiative. When "Viscount Morrow" glanced at the wife, she seemed content to leave the negotiation to her husband, resting her hands holding the closed fan upon her lap and smiling with an air of reassurance.

If that was how it would be, then "Viscount Morrow" conveyed that he would prepare whatever was necessary to earn the Schwartz couple's interest and understanding.


"The financial details of your enterprises are extremely important. We would of course like to review the documents, but more than that, what my wife and I place the greatest importance upon is whether the counterparty is worthy of trust. –Recently, I have heard that your lordship has dismissed many long-serving retainers and appointed new personnel."


At the topic raised, irritation toward the unnecessary retainers swelled within "Viscount Morrow's" heart.

It was a concern that would be eliminated tonight, but that did not diminish his anger toward the nuisances. Especially if they might become obstacles to incorporating new, useful talent.


"Though one should not speak of achievements and ability in the same breath, dismissing those who have served long is no easy decision. Surely, there were those who had been at your lordship's side since your youth."


What they wanted to know, apparently, was how they would be treated after negotiations succeeded and they safely became business partners.

They seemed unable to stop wondering how this "Viscount Morrow," who had so ruthlessly cut off his old retainers for the sake of change, would make use of those he newly brought into his fold.

An unnecessary worry. If they proved useful, he would not dispose of them carelessly. Though, if they proved too useful and were deemed usable as "poison," he could not deny the possibility that they might be replaced with serviceable pawns, just like "Viscount Morrow" himself.


In any case, hoping to dispel the Schwartz couple's doubts, "Viscount Morrow" spoke with visible emotion of his memories and the achievements of the retainers he had been forced, weeping, to let go.

None of the memories were fabrications; they were all true events. Regarding the story of the injury from falling off a dragon, he could even answer the location of the fall, the injuries sustained, and the faces and names of all the servants involved.

Only by being capable of that much could he finally fulfill his duty—not as a placebo, but as true "poison."


"――I see. I understand thoroughly."


To "Viscount Morrow," who had tearfully spoken of his bonds with his retainers, the husband nodded, deeply moved. His amber eyes glanced toward his wife seated beside him.

So the division of roles was that the husband handled negotiation while the wife held decision-making authority. At least, if they had been listening to his answers thus far, there should be no option other than to nod in agreement—,


"May I confirm just one more thing?"


"Natsumi?"


Perhaps this was not part of their prepared plan, for the husband's brow furrowed slightly at his wife's words.

To the questioning eye of the eyepatch-covered husband, the wife responded with a gesture of pressing her folded fan beneath her chin, her dark eyes reflecting "Viscount Morrow" as she asked,


"The linga tree in the garden bore fruit again this year. –What is the meaning of this?"


It was a question from a completely different angle, unlike anything in the exchange thus far.

It was not about Disken Morrow's abilities or the validity of his personal relationships, but rather something concerning his most private memories—and why she had uttered it was curious indeed.

But at that moment, "Viscount Morrow," as if to conceal that he had been caught off guard by the question's content, etched a small, wry smile onto his lips and shook his head.


What Natsumi Schwartz had spoken of was a memory shared with a retainer who had long served "Viscount Morrow" and retired several years ago—a trivial episode in which the real viscount, served linga at that retainer's home, had found it astringent and promised sweet linga every year thereafter.

Four days ago, a letter from that retainer had contained the same wording. "Viscount Morrow" had confirmed its meaning with the real one that very day, filling in the gaps in his memory.

Since then, it seemed linga had been sent every year. This year too, the sender would believe that Viscount Morrow would receive it, and would send linga.


"My... so it was a lovely invitation understood only between the two of you."


Hearing this, Natsumi raised a hand to her eyes, dabbing at the corners as if deeply moved. Judging by her reaction just now, her wariness toward "Viscount Morrow" had completely melted away.

Of course, even if there were a hundred or two hundred more questions, it would not matter. The answers that the real viscount would give, "Viscount Morrow" could produce perfectly—,


"――A false wind is blowing."


When that clear, dignified voice rang out, "Viscount Morrow's" thoughts froze for an instant.

As he raised his cup to his lips to moisten them, frozen in place, he saw in his vision the husband—who had uttered those words—raise a hand to his own face and remove the eyepatch.

The exposed left eye——it was a dazzling golden eye. The "Dragon's Eye."


"――!"


The instant he sensed danger, "Viscount Morrow" kicked up the table between himself and the couple.

With a deafening crash, the small table rose to block the space between the viscount and the couple. But it was silently bisected by a drawn blade of light, and the long leg thrust from beyond could not be stopped.

The sole of the smoothly extending kick struck him in the chest, knocking the breath from him, and "Viscount Morrow's" body was sent flying straight backward. But falling backward and rolling was exactly what he had intended.


Keeping his opponent in view, he drew the staff hidden behind the backrest of the sofa where he had been seated, and aimed the tip of the magic staff—set with a magic crystal—at the insolent couple—no, at the great enemy.

The being bearing the "Dragon's Eye"—"Viscount Morrow" fully understood that such was the enemy of the "poison." Therefore, without holding back, a blade of wind swelled—,


"――E・M・T!" "Yes!"


The explosive wind that should have surged forth to shred the great enemy's body approached.

But the blade of wind that should have been released from the magic staff suddenly lost its efficacy, transformed into a harmless something without meaning or form, and scattered. And at the same moment this mysterious phenomenon occurred, an even more incomprehensible sight burst into "Viscount Morrow's" view.


Behind the "Dragon's Eye" stood Natsumi Schwartz—and from beneath her voluminous skirt, which had been flipped up dramatically, emerged a young girl in a dress.

Surely she couldn't have been hiding in the skirt all along—bewilderment seized his mind. –That was the decisive blow.


"How unfortunate. It seems you were simply outmatched."


With a blade pressed to his neck, "Viscount Morrow" was immobilized. And it was not the "Dragon's Eye" holding the sword, but Natsumi Schwartz—for some reason triumphant—with the young girl at her side.



　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　△▼△▼△▼△



――Due to Viscount Disken Morrow's sudden illness, the banquet was closed early.


Leaving behind words of concern for the viscount's health, the guests withdrew one after another, and the orchestra prepared for the banquet also departed the manor, until at last the clamor of the evening celebration completely vanished.

Only then, surveying the people gathered in a secluded room on the first floor of the mansion, Subaru let out a small, relieved sigh.


"Truly, I am so relieved from the bottom of my heart that nothing happened to any of you..."


"Oi, how long are you gonna stay in character! You're way too good at it—is that supposed to be the specialty of a knight, huh!?"


To Subaru—still in her Natsumi Schwartz state, pressing a handkerchief to her eyes as if holding back tears of emotion—came a stream of abuse from the side: Rachins, dressed in a waiter's uniform.

Clad in the black-and-white attire reminiscent of a bartender or garçon, with his neatly styled hair, he looked quite like a respectable young man. Indeed, during the infiltration of the banquet, his conduct had reportedly been quite well received by the ladies.


"That said, it's those eyes of yours, isn't it. I understand, you see. From a young age, I too heard all sorts of things about my eyes."


"Don't go lumping me in with you. We just happened to cross paths. I got no need for guys who happily cross-dress as my comrades."


"Is that something you should be saying? Among your own faction, there might well be a gentleman who looks surprisingly good in women's clothing."


"As if! Tch... whether it's you or 'Blue Serpent'..."


"――Hm? Were you talking about me just now?"


Rachins' cheek stiffened as he twisted his lips and cursed. Glancing sideways, his sharp, white-eyed gaze fell upon Ferris, who had appeared through the door of an adjoining room, wearing the same waiter's uniform as him.

Having removed his jacket and rolled up his shirtsleeves, Ferris wiped the blood from his hands with a cloth while glaring at Rachins and, for some reason, Subaru.


"Good work, 'Blue Serpent.' Are the injured alright?"


"――. Well, barely. The people gathered in the annex... those who'd become inconvenient after the replacement were about to be executed en masse, but 'Long Tongue' and the others stopped it. Even so, the first one chosen was in a dangerous state."


"He managed to pull through from that? As usual, your magic is monstrous."


"I'll take that as a compliment. If 'Long Tongue' ever ends up on the brink of death, I'll be sure to heal him right up—since I'm such a monster, after all."


Ferris did not soften the sarcasm in response to the loose-tongued Rachins.

Smiling wryly at the two who couldn't quite get along, Subaru watched as the anxious people—the former retainers of Disken Morrow who had been spared execution—were filled with relief. Hearing that their critically injured comrade had been saved, they were finally beginning to feel that they were truly safe.

Just then—,


"Kahahaha, what a merry and uncooperative bunch! If you've been saved, then rejoice and make merry! If you've got worries, then weep and wail! No room for wishy-washy half-measures!"


Cutting through the atmosphere of relief with ill-timed water was a voice letting out a great laugh.

Stomping heavily across the floor and peering into the room without any reserve was a tall young man of valiant bearing, with dark skin and unruly wheat-colored hair. His face—which would have been dashing if composed—was twisted into a deeply mocking expression, and he was cheerfully drinking from a bottle of wine in his hand.

He was supposed to be wearing the same waiter's uniform as Ferris and the others, but his behavior and mannerisms made it seem like an entirely different outfit—such was the strange impression he gave.

Of course, that reaction was only from Subaru and the others, who were used to his attitude. For people who had just barely been saved from mortal danger, it was far too stimulating.


"Honestly, 'Black Dog,' what is with that attitude? Toward people who were in such a perilous position, how can you be so inconsiderate..."


"Oho, what's this, a lecture? But! Before you go on about my attitude, how about taking a look at your own appearance, hiding the truth like that? And on top of this, that, and the other, I'm their lifesaver, you know. Ain't got no reason to be complained at, do I?"


"That and this are separate matters..."


"If you're gonna split things up freely with your logic, then I'll match things conveniently with my own. There—if you wanna comfort 'em, go right ahead. I'll keep drinking on my own. Though if the beautiful duchess would pour for me, that'd be more than welcome. Kahahaha!"


When both sides appeal to their own logic, it's hard to press the point against "Black Dog," who had produced results.

In fact, without his cooperation, the damage caused by the "replacement dolls"—for which no other word than villainous would suffice—carried out in Viscount Disken Morrow's manor could not have been reduced to zero. –No, even now, it could not be said that it had truly been reduced to zero.


"Unfortunately, we found no reactions suggesting such anywhere in the manor. As expected, we should consider that the real one is imprisoned somewhere else."


Just as Subaru quietly clenched her fist, Beatrice returned with a report that confirmed exactly that concern.

Beside her—having accompanied her in searching for any magical traces or mechanisms throughout the manor—stood the beauty, Crusch.

Crusch, who had played the role of Natsumi Schwartz's husband, had already reattached her eyepatch, and with her gallant, handsome features, confirmed Beatrice's report with, "Indeed."


"I circled the manor with 'Librarian,' but nothing abnormal caught my eye either. The one impersonating Disken Morrow shows no sign of talking. If any of his subordinates had survived, it might be a different story..."


"Kahahaha, too bad, so sad, my condolences."


Even under Crusch's sharp gaze, "Black Dog's" attitude did not falter.

When hiding his identity with a mask and robe, he had already given the impression of someone who toyed with others—but even after removing them and revealing a face that didn't match his personality at all, his eccentric impression only grew stronger.

At any rate—,


"――So our lord is nowhere to be found, is he."


The one who uttered those words in a hoarse voice, before the assembled group, was one of the old retainers seated in a chair.

The viscount's retainers who had been summoned to the annex and nearly executed numbered fourteen in all. Every one of them could be called true loyalists who had sensed something wrong with the fake Viscount Morrow—the victim of the "replacement doll."

The others, too, surely shared the same concern for their lord.


"...'Long Tongue.'"


"Tch, sorry to tell you all, but we searched not just this mansion, but the annex and the basement too. Those two checked for hidden rooms and whatnot as well. But it was a bust."


"Then our master..."


The doubts were confirmed as fact—the real one, whose seat had been stolen by the fake, was nowhere to be found. At this, expressions of disappointment and resignation began to spread over the faces of the old retainers.

But Subaru said, "Wait," and spoke up,


"It's too early to give up. I confirmed it with the fake. –About the story of the linga tree, between Viscount Morrow and someone who once worked at the manor."


"That's the story with my predecessor as steward? But..."


"He is our collaborator. And the letter about that matter was written four days ago... moreover, the fake answered it perfectly. Isn't that right, 'Jade Beauty'?"


"Indeed. There was no lie in those words. Not his own memory, but the story itself was certainly true. –I see. So that was why you added an unplanned question."


"Yes. –Do you understand? The fake knew a memory that he could only have learned from the real Viscount Morrow. Which means..."


"――Our master is alive?"


"――Yes, there's no mistake."


Before the wide-eyed old retainer, Subaru stepped forward, took his wrinkled, dry hands, and nodded deeply. Immediately, tears welled in the old man's eyes, and the emotion spread to those around him.

With that as the trigger, the atmosphere of the room—which had been on the verge of being dominated by heavy gloom—changed.

No one cried aloud, but the faces that had been on the verge of hanging in despair lifted one after another, and in their eyes dwelled a sense of mission—to search for their missing lord.


"If that is so, then we must find our master at all costs—"


"――Your sentiments are just. But precisely because you think of your lord, I cannot recommend that course of action."


"――!"


But it was the cross-dressing Crusch who halted those feelings of the old retainers head-on.

Just as when she had apprehended the fake who had impersonated "Viscount Morrow" at the banquet, she touched the longsword at her waist and surveyed the old retainers with a dignified presence.

Under her sharp gaze, they trembled, asking, "Why...?"


"At present, this territory is without its lord. Among those appointed by the fake, some followed without knowing the truth, while others may have been in league with the enemy. If the chain of command is not reorganized, governance kept running, and the impact on the people minimized, how much chaos would result?"


"That is..."


"When the real Disken Morrow returns, if the territory is in ruin, the people suffering, and nothing left of what the viscount should protect—then that could not truly be called the salvation of one's lord."


"――――"


As Crusch continued speaking, the old retainers—who had initially shown confusion and resistance—began to display understanding and acceptance on their faces, naturally lending their ears to her argument.

This was proof of the natural charisma she possessed—the ability to make others listen, to lead—,


"From here on, what you must do is maintain the appearance of the territory exactly as it has been. So that the elimination of the fake is not discovered, govern jointly through council, and spread word that the viscount is bedridden with illness. –For the sake of your lord who will one day return, protect that seat—the place where the viscount belongs."


Thinking of the weight of those words coming from Crusch's lips, Subaru gazed at her profile.

There was no personal sentiment in that noble profile as she faced the old retainers and delivered her dignified words. Yet, she too was someone who had been stripped of her position by false charges and forced to remain a fugitive.


"Again, saying such things..."


The one who murmured that quietly from behind was Ferris—the one who could not help but harbor the strongest, most impulsive feelings regarding Crusch's current situation. But it did not reach Crusch, who faced the loyal retainers as they struck their own frail cheeks with their hands and nodded deeply.


"――Understood. Until our master returns, we shall protect this land, and his seat, without fail. Therefore..."


"Yes, leave it to us."


To the resolute words of the old retainers, Crusch nodded deeply in return.

And then—,


"――Even if every drop of blood in this body is drained, I swear to cut out the disease that has afflicted this kingdom, and to clear away your worries."


Thus, despite being stripped of her position, she answered with a pride perhaps unmatched among the royal selection candidates—a pride fitting of exactly what they ought to be.



　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　△▼△▼△▼△



"――Everyone, excellent work! With no casualties on our side, the mission was a success."


"Well, with Betty and Subaru on the job, this much was only natural. 'White Sheep' did quite well too, didn't she? I'll praise you, you know."


"Th-thank you very much. I'm, um, honored!"


Bowing repeatedly and beaming bashfully at Beatrice's words of appreciation was "White Sheep," who had played a particularly significant role in this operation.

Dressed not in her usual black suit but in a pale blue evening gown for infiltrating the banquet, she had slipped into the celebration as a guest—just like the Schwartz couple played by Subaru and Crusch—and, using her affable nature, had skillfully steered the topics of conversation among the attendees so that the fake "Viscount Morrow" would take an interest in the Schwartz couple.


"Of course, we tried to handle things as smoothly as possible too, but 'White Sheep's' competence was astonishing. Truly, a big help."


"N-no, it was thanks to Subaru-san... I mean, Natsumi-san and the others. I really didn't do anything special..."


"Saying you didn't do anything special is a lie, isn't it. Also, I've already taken off the wig and removed the makeup, so you can call me Subaru, not Natsumi. I don't want to call myself Natsumi in an incomplete state."


"Eh, but you're still wearing the dress... Ah, never mind. Yes."


Subaru tilted her head at "White Sheep," who had started to say something and then stopped. But rather than pursuing it, the fatigue from the release of tension hit her all at once.


"Honestly, it went almost too well..."


So thinking, Subaru—still in her dress—looked back toward the direction of Viscount Morrow's manor, where the old retainers had continued bowing to see them off until the very end.

What had happened in that manor tonight would be kept secret, and until the day the real viscount returned, the territory would be maintained through the efforts of his retainers.

However—,


"I'll say it now—those folks have probably realized it too. The possibility that the enemy catches on and kills the real viscount."


"Rachins..."


"Don't go saying something all dramatic like they've entrusted it to us. As if we can just go around carrying other people's baggage. We're already full up with your baggage as it is."


At Rachins' tongue-clicking, Subaru could find no words to reply.

Whether this Viscount Morrow domain could truly be restored to its original state depended on clinging to a slender thread of hope and believing in miracles as one crossed a rickety bridge.

And believing in the miracle of that bridge was not limited to Viscount Morrow's domain alone.


"It's already been two months..."


As if carving regret and impatience into herself, Subaru let her thoughts turn to the time that had passed. –Two months had elapsed since Subaru and the others had been driven from the royal capital and had joined forces with "Six Tongues."

During that time, while traveling from place to place throughout the kingdom, the battle against the "Beloved Children"—who had infiltrated deep into the Kingdom of Lugunica and truly sought to overthrow the nation—had continued.

What had astonished them was that the replacement of influential figures by the "Beloved Children" extended not only to the upper echelons of the royal capital, but throughout the entire kingdom. –Cases like tonight's Viscount Morrow were the sixth that Subaru and the others had resolved in these two months, having already dealt with five prior.


"The kingdom's damage from the 'replacement dolls' is serious. I understand that crushing them one by one has meaning. I understand, but..."


"Subaru, impatience is forbidden. Emilia and the others at the castle are surely safe. Crusch has guaranteed that much, hasn't she?"


"――To be precise, it's a process where our people remaining at the castle confirm it, and 'Jade Beauty' verifies through her blessing that I am not falsifying that report."


"――――"


Beatrice placed her small hands over Subaru's clenched fist, nestling close to her heart. Then, a figure who had appeared from the building interjected into their exchange.

In the forest away from the Morrow manor, at the entrance of a hunting lodge that "Six Tongues" had secured as a temporary gathering place, stood Russell Fellow—the chief of the organization in question.


"Russell-san, did you come all the way out personally to greet us?"


"Yes. 'White Sheep,' who was sent to welcome you, never returned, so I wondered if some unforeseen situation had occurred. Also, during operations, I would ask that you call me 'Silver Fox,' 'Gray Star.'"


"Hm, are we still considered to be in the middle of the operation?"


"At the very least, until you have changed out of that dress, the captured fake Disken Morrow has been transported, and we have safely departed this area—until such a determination can be made."


At Russell's extremely cautious reply, Subaru scratched her cheek with a finger.

Optimistic opinions like "you're overthinking it" or "we don't need to go that far" were not things Subaru could voice, having seen the enemy's movements thus far. Russell's thoroughgoing policy was, in fact, what had kept "Six Tongues"—and by extension, the kingdom—alive.


"Still, leaving aside my 'Gray Star' and Beako's 'Librarian,' Rachins' 'Long Tongue' feels a bit too half-hearted, don't you think? Though my suggestion for Crusch-san's 'Jade Beauty' did get accepted."


"The requirement is that they be obvious yet not obvious. Everyone, please come inside. There are matters regarding our future direction that I would like to discuss. –Ah, yes, and 'Gray Star.'"


"Yes?"


"Although it ultimately did not affect the operation, inserting unnecessary exchanges dramatically increased the risk of arousing the opponent's suspicion. Please refrain from such conduct without due consideration. It is an act that endangers all of us—that is, the kingdom itself."


At the warning delivered with narrowed eyes and a quiet tone, Subaru let out a small groan.

The criticism was, frankly, something Subaru had done knowingly. It had been Subaru who had asked the former steward of Viscount Morrow—the one who became their informant—to write the story about the linga tree in his letter.

All of it was because, when asked about information the fake wouldn't know, she wanted to be certain that the fake had been in contact with the information source—that is, the real one, who was being kept alive.

And what would be saved by that information was not something as grand as the Kingdom of Lugunica, which Russell prioritized—,


"What was necessary was to save those retainers, who would have hung their heads in despair at the loss of their lord's life in that moment."


It was Crusch, present at the scene, who revealed the true intent behind Subaru's actions from behind.

She herself could be said to have been the one drawn into the most dangerous situation by Subaru's unilateral decision. Of course, so was Beatrice, who had been hiding in the skirt holding her breath.


"'Jade Beauty,' are you affirming his actions?"


"If we are to place weight on rationality, then 'Gray Star's' unilateral action cannot be called praiseworthy. From the perspective of a duchess as well, it could be said to have been an erroneous judgment. –However."


"However?"


"I am now a fugitive from the nation, stripped of my position as duchess. Moreover, by my personal, subjective assessment, I find this man's softness rather likable. You have my deepest sympathies for the burden placed upon you."


"...I will take your opinion under advisement."


At Crusch's brazen favoritism toward Subaru, Russell rubbed the corner of his eye and responded thus.


"I apologize, but allow me to go inside. As expected, using this eye takes a heavy toll. I won't fall asleep, but I would like to sit down."


Leaving those words behind, Crusch passed by Russell and entered the hunting lodge. "Black Dog," who had been indifferent to the conversation, had already entered the building as well, and "White Sheep" hurriedly bowed before dashing inside—probably to prepare tea for everyone.


"Hah, I get the urge to say all kinds of things. So feel free to spout whatever you want, I won't mind. No matter how much of an idiot you are, you wouldn't do it without some kind of plan."


Laughing at the scolded Subaru, Rachins slung his removed jacket over his shoulder and entered the lodge. To those words—impossible to tell whether they were encouragement or sympathy—Russell did not respond. Instead, he looked at Ferris behind Subaru and the others, and said,


"'Blue Serpent,' come inside too. You may be tired, but..."


"Not particularly? The numbers and the severity of the injuries were far better than the worst times. If I got tired from this much, I'd be useless at the healing institute in the royal capital."


As Ferris waved a hand dismissively, Russell shrugged and returned to the lodge.

As for Subaru, she was aware that she had contributed to Russell's stress, so honestly, she was full of the desire to apologize.


"Though it would probably just get on his nerves even more..."


"It can't be helped. It's the duty of those who stand above others, I suppose. Emilia and Roswaal are the exceptions, being utterly removed from that kind of anguish."


"I guess so. And honestly, if I told him the whole truth, it'd probably just make Russell-san angrier."


Understanding that Russell was making every effort to accommodate them, Subaru still couldn't stop herself from unilaterally demanding more.

The prime example of that was today's unilateral decision, and although Rachins had spoken as he did—,


"In reality, you had no plan at all, did you."


"Ferris..."


"It's 'Blue Serpent.' 'Silver Fox' would be annoyed if he heard, 'Gray Star'-kun, 'Librarian'-chan."


"――――"


"'Gray Star'-kun is really good at being indulged, you know. –I'm nothing but envious."


With a cute voice and a gaze of un-cute sharpness, Subaru was left speechless.

But without allowing any further response, Ferris too passed by Subaru and entered the hunting lodge, leaving only Subaru and Beatrice standing there.


"Hah... I'm seriously doing my best, aren't I, Beako."


"Subaru's efforts are always watched over from the closest place by Betty. ...I'm sure everyone is fine. They're a tough bunch who don't stay down, after all."


"――Right. We're not in any position to be complaining."


Even if through Russell, the safety of Emilia and Ram, who remained at the castle, had been barely confirmed. However, the whereabouts of Rem, Otto, and the others—who had apparently fled—remained unknown.

Still, the negative trust in an enemy who would surely announce it loudly if they were captured was, indirectly, conveying their safety—or so Subaru convinced herself.

In any case—,


"――Seriously, it's not just Emilia-ase I'm missing—Rem-cithin, Ram-noic acid, Garf-ein, Otto-gen, Petra-mide, Mei-lytol, and Freder-min too! And while I'm at it, Roswa-nol as well!"


"It can't be helped. For now, you'll just have to make do with Beatrol-min, won't you?"


"Uohhh, Beatrol-min absorption—! Sucking up Beako!"


Thinking of the companions she couldn't see, her feelings for them growing ever stronger, Subaru lifted Beatrice up, hugged her light body tightly, and spun around and around.

Spinning, she prayed to the twinkling stars above. –For their safety, and for reunion.


"'Gray Star'! 'Librarian'! I told you to come inside! Don't make me repeat myself!"


With that, the scolding voice of Russell—heard countless times over these two months—rang out high into the night sky above the forest.
